  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and humid conditions prevail this afternoon\nwith NW flow keeping the sea breeze pinned to the coast.\nTemperatures have reached the mid 90s away from the immediate \ncoast with upper 80s to lower 90s along the coast. Dew point\ntemps in the mid to upper 70s have allowed heat indices to \nclimb to around the 105-115 degree range. The Extreme Heat\nWarning and Heat Advisory remains in effect through 00z this\nevening. \n\nWednesday will see slight relief from the heat with a frontal \nboundary stalling just off the coast overnight bringing N to NE\nwinds into Wednesday morning before the sea breeze becomes\ndominant later in the afternoon. Temps expected in the upper \n80s/lower 90s northern tier with heat indices peaking around \n100-105, however southern sections of the forecast area will see\ntemps reach the mid 90s with heat indices around 105-110 and \nhave issued a Heat Advisory for Pamlico, Craven, Carteret, \nJones, Onslow and Duplin Counties. A return to S/SW flow across \nthe area Thursday will bring a greater area of heat indices \naround 105-110 and will likely see another Heat Advisory for \nmuch of the area. A frontal boundary drops into the are Friday\nbringing greater cloud coverage as well as coverage of storms \nand expect Heat Indices to remain below criteria. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...NW flow has kept the sea breeze pinned to the\ncoast this afternoon with limited convection across ENC so far,\nhowever a few thunderstorms are beginning to develop across \nMainland Dare Co. Additionally, widely scattered thunderstorms \nare pushing across NE NC associated with a subtle shortwave and\nweak cold front moving north of the area. Convection this\nafternoon is generally expected to be confined to coastal\nsections along the sea breeze and northern counties as the weak\nfront drops into the area and should dissipate during the\nevening with loss of heating. The front weakens with the sea \nbreeze becoming dominant and making a farther push inland \nWednesday afternoon. Precip chances generally remain around \n20-40% before chances decrease after sunset. Despite poor shear,\nhigh PWATs exceeding 2.25\" are expected and a stronger cell \ncould pose an isolated wet microburst threat. Otherwise, overall\nsevere threat is low - less than 5%.\n\nContinued moist and unstable on Thursday with a mid level\nshortwave pushing across the region bringing a slight increase\nin shear to around 25 kt which may allow for stronger storms.\nSPC has a marginal risk (Level 1 of 5) for severe storms with\ndamaging wind gusts the primary severe threat. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Deeper trough begins to dig across the Ohio\nRiver Valley on Thursday and push towards the Eastern Seaboard \non Friday and into the weekend with a cold front stalling across\nthe region. Favorable jet dynamics, moist SSW flow aloft along\nwith a series of shortwave trough the upper level trough will\nbring high chances of showers and thunderstorms late in the week\nand into the weekend. Greater cloud coverage will likely limited\nstrong instability and storms are generally expected to be sub-\nsevere, though heavy rainfall will continue to be a threat with\nPW values greater than 2\".",
